{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Let there be a firmament</b> — The material that came into being at the beginning, which He created from nothing, He now commanded that it become a stretched-out firmament like a tent in the midst of the waters, and that it separate between waters and waters. And perhaps this is what they intended when they said (Genesis Rabbah 4:2): Rav said: the heavens were moist on the first day, and on the second day they congealed. Rav said: יְהִי רָקִיעַ means 'let the firmament strengthen.' Rabbi Yehudah son of Rabbi Simon said: let the firmament be made into a cloth, as you say (Exodus 39:3): 'And they hammered out (וַיְרַקְּעוּ) plates of gold' etc." "<b>In the midst of the waters</b> — In the middle of the waters, between the upper waters and the lower waters. Just as there is a separation between the firmament and the waters that are upon the earth, so there is a separation between the upper waters and the firmament — thus you have learned that they are suspended by the utterance [of the divine command]. Genesis Rabbah (4:3), and Rashi cited it (Rashi on Genesis 1:6). And this matter belongs to the Account of the Beginning, and do not expect me to write anything about it, for the subject is among the secrets of the Torah. Nor do the verses require this explanation, for Scripture does not elaborate on its matter, and the interpretation is forbidden to those who know it, and all the more so to us."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-21T22:27:10.472802091Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}